WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guest is
a talented actress

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.400
you know from films such as
"Mad Max: Fury Road"

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.934
and "Magic Mike"
and shows like

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:08.000
"The Girlfriend Experience"
and "The Terminal List."

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.267
She stars in
"Daisy Jones &amp; The Six,"

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.567
which begins streaming
on Prime Video this Friday.

00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:13.801
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.534
-What can I do for you?

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:17.834
-I don't know.

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:20.968
-What do you want?
-I want to make a record.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:22.834
-Okay.
-And I want people

00:00:22.834 --> 00:00:25.367
to listen to it over and over
again until it breaks.

00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:29.200
And then I want to make
another one.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.567
-Well, you got one great song.
All we need now is 10 more.

00:00:36.968 --> 00:00:39.234
-Give me a week.

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:41.100
-[Bleep ]

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.300
♪♪

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.734
-Oh, and, Daisy,

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:48.667
this is now the worst song
on your album.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:52.801
♪♪

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:55.567
-Please welcome back to the show
Riley Keough, everybody!

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.467
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:06.300
♪♪

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.133
♪♪

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.934
Welcome back!
-Thank you!

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:15.100
I just grabbed your hand.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.067
I didn't know if you were
offering or introducing.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.801
-It was like,
"And this is the couch."

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:22.501
I did. I did want you to --

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:24.300
I wanted you to use it
as you saw fit.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.501
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:26.934
I think it went
very smoothly.

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.701
It's a little awkward how much
we're talking about it now,

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:29.968
but...
-"Stop."

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:33.701
-Hey, so this is -- because
this is a show about a band,

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:37.601
and also there's an album
that has come out.

00:01:37.601 --> 00:01:40.367
It must be so different than any
other show you're promoting,

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.467
because it does also feel like
you're promoting as a musician.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:46.968
-Totally. It's the first time
I've done anything musical.

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:49.067
That's been its own experience.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:50.734
But we had our album
come out today.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:52.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:56.067
-It's wonderful, too.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:59.501
Obviously you have --
you come from a musical family,

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:03.033
but I'm surprised that you've
never done anything musical.

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.300
-Yeah, I mean, I played, like,
a little bit of piano

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.067
when I was a kid and
played around with it.

00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:10.501
But I just never was interested.
-That's so funny.

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:12.968
-I know. I love it now.
You know?

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:15.033
-So did -- I mean --

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:17.734
Did the casting process sort of
have to take a leap of faith

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.534
that you were gonna eventually
be able to figure it out?

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:23.000
-Yes.
I think that I oversold myself

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.767
a little bit in the beginning
of the process.

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:26.667
-That's very nice that
you're admitting it now, yeah.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:27.834
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:30.400
No, I -- it was something

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.234
I just really wanted to prove
to myself that I could do,

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.200
I think, ultimately, and
I think they saw that in me

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.000
and they wanted
to give me a chance.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:40.634
-But you did take it seriously.
And I hope --

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:43.367
Is it correct to say
that you and your cast

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:46.334
went to a band camp of sorts?
-Yeah.

00:02:46.334 --> 00:02:48.801
Band camp, we would start it
at like 9:00 in the morning.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.868
We would do singing lessons
and guitar lessons

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:54.133
and piano lessons
and full band practice

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:57.200
and go over to the other side,
at Sound City Studios in L.A.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.000
and, like, record our album.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.767
So it was like a full, like,
Rock -- School of Rock.

00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:06.334
-Were other cast members maybe
a little bit farther along

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:08.000
in their education than you?
Or did you --

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.267
-Yes.
-Okay, got it.

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:11.133
-Everyone in our band
was a musician,

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.400
other than Sam and myself,
who are the two lead singers.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.100
So it was, like --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.601
It was, like, crazy.
But it was --

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:21.834
What was amazing is the first --
the first time

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:25.601
we ever rehearsed together,
I was super nervous.

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:27.934
Because we'd been --
we'd been practicing alone

00:03:27.934 --> 00:03:29.400
and getting our lessons
by ourselves.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.467
And I was thinking, you know,
like, I'm the worst one here.

00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:35.300
And then I met Sam.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.868
No, and then we had our first
singing lesson together.

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:41.167
And there was this moment where
we were looking at each other,

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:42.868
and they kind of, like,
wanted us to sing

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:43.901
really close
to each other's face

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:45.300
and, like, build chemistry,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.567
the first time
we'd ever met each other.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:50.567
And we both realized, "Oh,
we're both, like, awful."

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.334
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:55.634
-And from then on, I just felt,
like, super grateful to have --

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.534
that we were both kind of
starting from the bottom.

00:03:57.534 --> 00:03:59.567
-When you meet -- again,

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:01.067
I think
a lot of romance starts

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:02.467
when you meet someone
as awful as you are.

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:04.601
-Right? Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:07.734
-Now, then you finished --
I guess there's this conclusion

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.300
of band camp.
At some point,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.968
you have to move on
and start doing the show.

00:04:10.968 --> 00:04:12.467
-Yeah.
-And I heard you had

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:15.033
a concert of sorts
for friends and family?

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:17.901
-We did.
There was no friends or family.

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:20.067
It was very --
It was a lot of executives.

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:21.767
-Okay. So the people...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:22.868
-I think --
-They're like,

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:24.267
"Please call us
'friends and family.'"

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.334
-I think they wanted it to feel
like friends or family.

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:30.200
We walked in the room, and it
was just, like, a corporate...

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.601
-How many people would you say
were there in your audience

00:04:32.601 --> 00:04:34.868
for your concert?
-I think I blacked out.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.234
But it might have been, like --
I feel like

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:38.701
I've said that
in every interview.

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:40.667
People think I have
a drinking problem.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:45.701
It must have been like --
I want to say it was 50, 60.

00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:47.467
-Oh, that's a real audience,
yeah.

00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:50.067
-You know, it was --
It was -- Yeah.

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:52.501
-It does seem like at least one
thing you have going for you is,

00:04:52.501 --> 00:04:54.767
again, because this is a cast
where you also play a band --

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.834
-I want to hear the one thing.
-You have one thing.

00:04:56.834 --> 00:04:58.734
No, but it just seems like
you guys

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:00.734
feel like a very close cast.
-We are.

00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:02.868
-I see photos of
you guys doing press.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.767
And I don't know,
maybe it's because

00:05:04.767 --> 00:05:07.234
you are playing people
in a band together,

00:05:07.234 --> 00:05:10.400
but you actually look like you
have a nice group chemistry.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.501
-We do.
And I think that's, you know,

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:14.100
been the most special thing
about the show is

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.501
that all of us are, you know --
it's not often

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:20.968
you get to do a series
with 10 people your age.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.534
-Yeah.
-And all get along well.

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:26.567
And I think that that sort of
translates into the show.

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:28.000
And that's been,
you know, authentic.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.801
-This is a show
about a band

00:05:29.801 --> 00:05:31.434
and what a band goes through.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:33.868
You obviously have
a career as an actor.

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:36.434
Do you -- Is there anything
about band life

00:05:36.434 --> 00:05:38.767
that feels more alluring
to you now that you wish --

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:40.100
-100%.
-Okay.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.200
-The -- I realized,
in making this show

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.567
and recording the album
that, like, our actors' job

00:05:46.567 --> 00:05:49.100
is very, like,
serious and stressful,

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.868
and they're very laid-back
and very easy-going.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:54.400
I remember going
into the studio,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.834
and we're always, like,
"Got to be on time,

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.133
got to be punctual,"
and everyone's late.

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:01.767
And I go to --
And then, you know --

00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:03.968
And it's -- to them, it's not.
It's like 15 minutes.

00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:05.767
To me, coming from film,
I'm like, "Oh, my God,

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:08.133
it's a disaster."
-Right.

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:09.767
-You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:12.868
And, like, we get in the studio,
and I remember

00:06:12.868 --> 00:06:15.033
being like, "Okay,
what time's lunch?"

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:18.400
And they were like, I mean,
"Whenever you're hungry."

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.767
And I was like, "Aah!"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.767 --> 00:06:25.767
I think I need, like, structure.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.767 --> 00:06:28.100
But then I, like,
relaxed into it.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.701
And I was like,
"This is so much cooler."

00:06:29.701 --> 00:06:31.000
-That's great.
-Yeah.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.133
-I don't know why you ran
from it when you were a kid.

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:34.434
-I know.
-Well, that's alright.

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:35.734
It's very nice that you
found your way back to it.

00:06:35.734 --> 00:06:37.767
-I did.
-So, I know

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:40.033
you met your husband on set.
-Yeah.

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:42.534
-You guys were working
on "Mad Max" together.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:46.000
And then he has a cameo
in this show.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.901
-He sure does.
-Now, tell us --

00:06:47.901 --> 00:06:49.934
This Is a wonderful story
how this cameo came about.

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:52.868
-Well, the cameo came about
[Chuckles]

00:06:52.868 --> 00:06:56.200
because I have to have
intercourse...

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.367
-Uh-huh?
-...with a random person

00:06:58.367 --> 00:06:59.701
in the show.
-Yeah.

00:06:59.701 --> 00:07:02.267
-And the producers were like,
"It would be so funny

00:07:02.267 --> 00:07:03.868
if it was your husband."
-That's so nice.

00:07:03.868 --> 00:07:05.767
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:08.133
And so he agrees?
-Yeah.

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:10.033
-Now, though, you're on set
with your husband,

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.701
you know,
simulating intercourse.

00:07:12.701 --> 00:07:15.400
Was that more or less awkward?
-It was more awkward.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.868
-Yeah, I bet.
-It was more awkward.

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:18.234
-It sounds good
in the beginning,

00:07:18.234 --> 00:07:20.434
but as I think about it --
-I think that they were thinking

00:07:20.434 --> 00:07:21.767
it would be less awkward,
and then I --

00:07:21.767 --> 00:07:23.100
and then we got there
and like, "This is

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.234
really uncomfortable."
-Yeah.

00:07:25.234 --> 00:07:27.400
-I mean, you don't --
like, for one,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.400
I've never had pretend sex
with my husband.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.801
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.801 --> 00:07:33.934
That's true.
-You know?

00:07:33.934 --> 00:07:37.033
And for two, you know --
normally, typically,

00:07:37.033 --> 00:07:38.400
when you do these things,
and it's, like,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.234
somebody coming in for the day,
you're like, "Hello.

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:41.601
Nice to meet you," and
you kind of

00:07:41.601 --> 00:07:43.767
get down to business,
and it's very professional.

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:46.234
But with him,
it was just like, really,

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:47.868
like we were just giggling
the whole time.

00:07:47.868 --> 00:07:49.167
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:51.367
-I would be so worried
if I had to have pretend sex

00:07:51.367 --> 00:07:52.901
with my wife
in front of a bunch of people.

00:07:52.901 --> 00:07:55.567
She'd be like,
"He doesn't usually..."

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:58.434
[ Laughter ]
-That's what was --

00:07:58.434 --> 00:08:00.534
-"He's putting
a lot more attention..."

00:08:00.534 --> 00:08:03.467
[ Laughter ]
-That's what happened.

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:06.133
Because we were --
In this scene --

00:08:06.133 --> 00:08:09.801
and this is a spoiler alert --
in the scene, it's not --

00:08:09.801 --> 00:08:12.501
it's not like --
I'm not really enjoying it.

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:14.734
-Yeah.
-And I feel like

00:08:14.734 --> 00:08:17.434
I really kept feeling inclined
to be like, "In real life..."

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:20.901
[ Laughter ]
"...it's not like this."

00:08:20.901 --> 00:08:24.467
And I was like, "I'll just
keep that to myself."

00:08:24.467 --> 00:08:25.934
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:27.567
-That's good that you guys
found together

00:08:27.567 --> 00:08:30.534
that when you have real sex,
it should be with him,

00:08:30.534 --> 00:08:32.033
and pretend sex
should be with strangers.

00:08:32.033 --> 00:08:33.400
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.300
That's a very helpful message
to all married couples.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.300
-It was, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:41.000
-You also directed a film...
-Yeah.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.601
-...I guess, last year now.
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:45.634
-"War Pony" which you worked on
for a great long time, I know.

00:08:45.634 --> 00:08:47.200
-Yeah.
-This is a real process.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.367
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:50.434
-And it was at
the Cannes Film Festival.

00:08:50.434 --> 00:08:51.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:08:51.968 --> 00:08:54.267
-Did you enjoy directing?

00:08:54.267 --> 00:08:55.734
Is that something you think
you'll do again?

00:08:55.734 --> 00:08:58.200
-I -- "Enjoy" is
an interesting word.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.133
[ Laughter ]

00:09:00.133 --> 00:09:01.300
It was wild.
-By the way,

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:02.767
I like how honest
this answer is.

00:09:02.767 --> 00:09:06.868
-You're my last interview
of the week.

00:09:06.868 --> 00:09:08.300
And I'm just really
letting It all out.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.267
-You're going to be honest.
-Just, like...

00:09:10.267 --> 00:09:11.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:13.400
-I [Laughs] -- I, um --

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:14.834
I did enjoy it. It's wild.

00:09:14.834 --> 00:09:16.334
It's, like,
much more challenging than I --

00:09:16.334 --> 00:09:18.634
I think actors have this feeling

00:09:18.634 --> 00:09:20.067
that they can just,
like, direct.

00:09:20.067 --> 00:09:22.334
-Yeah.
-And it's really hard.

00:09:22.334 --> 00:09:23.767
-It is.
I mean, I think

00:09:23.767 --> 00:09:27.467
that you forget how exhausting
decision-making is.

00:09:27.467 --> 00:09:29.601
-Yes.
-Because, ultimately, I think

00:09:29.601 --> 00:09:32.200
every role on a film set
is tiring in its own way.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.167
-Yeah.
-But I think the amount of --

00:09:34.167 --> 00:09:36.667
even just tiny decisions
you have to make as a director,

00:09:36.667 --> 00:09:38.267
people always underestimate.
-Totally.

00:09:38.267 --> 00:09:41.834
Decisions, and then just, like,
the admin and the scheduling

00:09:41.834 --> 00:09:44.000
and just things that I'm totally
not interested in,

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.167
you know, that you have to do.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:09:46.167 --> 00:09:47.767
-But it was amazing. You know?

00:09:47.767 --> 00:09:50.334
And we were able to make
this very collaborative

00:09:50.334 --> 00:09:53.033
film experience
that was, you know --

00:09:53.033 --> 00:09:54.834
I'll remember it
for the rest of my life.

00:09:54.834 --> 00:09:56.501
-Well, congratulations on that.
-Thank you.

00:09:56.501 --> 00:09:58.467
-Congratulations on the show.
-Thank you.

00:09:58.467 --> 00:10:00.167
-You're discovering
your musical talent.

